  <title>Browse mode and focus mode</title>
  <p>
    Navigate document content and interact with controls.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browse mode lets Orca provide navigation for reading documents and web content. Focus mode
    lets the application handle its navigation keys instead. Other Orca commands, including
    table navigation and commands using the <key>Orca Modifier</key>, remain available.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unlike screen readers that use a virtual buffer, Orca does not create a separate, off-screen
    copy of a document or web page for browse mode. You navigate the content in the application
    itself. In browse mode, Orca handles navigation commands and updates your position within
    that content.
  </p>
  <p>
    Orca can switch between modes automatically as you navigate. The settings and commands below
    control automatic switching and let you choose a mode explicitly.
  </p>
  <section id="settings-browse-mode-and-focus-mode">
    <title>Settings</title>
    <section id="settings-browse-mode-and-focus-mode-document-presenter-native-navigation">
      <title>Native navigation</title>
      <p>
        <em style="strong">Preferences location:</em>
        <gui>Documents</gui> category, <gui>Native navigation</gui> group.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em style="strong">Available settings:</em>
      </p>
      <list>
        <item>
          <p>
            <gui>Automatic focus mode</gui>: Controls whether
            Orca switches to focus mode when native navigation, such as Tab or Shift+Tab, lands
            on an interactive widget. Default: on.
          </p>
        </item>
        <item>
          <p>
            <gui>Automatic sticky focus mode for web applications</gui>: Starts web
            applications, including desktop applications built with Electron, in sticky focus
            mode so their own keyboard interactions work. Default: on.
          </p>
        </item>
      </list>
    </section>
    <section id="settings-browse-mode-and-focus-mode-document-presenter-caret-navigation">
